    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d185">

                <pb facs="0375" n="249" sameAs="frus:frus1925v01/pg249"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">121.5768/1</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in Greece</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Laughlin</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to
                        the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Athens</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1925-10-06"><hi rendition="#italic">October 6,
                        1925</hi></date>.<lb/>[Received November 3.]</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 428</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: In reply to your instruction No. 189 of
                    April 21st,<anchor n="80" xml:id="d185-f1"/> in which you informed me that Mr.
                    James F. O’Neill had been attached to this Legation as Customs Attaché and in
                    which you directed me to request his recognition and those of his subordinates
                    in their respective capacities by the proper authorities, I have the honor to
                    report that I am to-day in receipt of an undated note from the Foreign Office in
                    which the Hellenic Government expresses its regret at not being able to accord
                    the recognition desired to these officials. A copy and translation of the note
                    will be found enclosed herewith.<anchor n="81" xml:id="d185-f2"/></p>
                <p>I have [etc.]</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Irwin Laughlin</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
